dup2 to a guarded FD returns EBADF instead of aborting the process.

Darling parks internal descriptors (ciderd RPC) at high fd numbers.
autoconf's dup2 check does dup2(1, RLIMIT_NOFILE) expecting EBADF, and the
RPC fd sits right there, so sys_dup2 aborted and killed the process (it broke
GNU hello's ./configure at the "whether dup2 works" test). macOS returns
EBADF for an out-of-range target, so do the same: the dup2 fails, the guarded
fd is preserved, and the process survives (close.c already declines to abort
on the same guard).

diff --git a/cider/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/unistd/dup2.c b/cider/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/unistd/dup2.c
index 62c0a2a..6a20ace 100644
--- a/cider/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/unistd/dup2.c
+++ b/cider/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/unistd/dup2.c
@@ -15,8 +15,16 @@ long sys_dup2(int fd_from, int fd_to)
 	int ret;
 
 	if (guard_table_check(fd_to, guard_flag_prevent_close)) {
-		__simple_kprintf("*** Someone tried to close a guarded FD (%d) via dup2! ***", fd_to);
-		__simple_abort();
+		// Darling parks internal descriptors (e.g. ciderd RPC) at high
+		// fd numbers. Programs that probe a "max/bad" fd can legitimately target
+		// one: autoconf's dup2 check does `dup2(1, RLIMIT_NOFILE)` expecting
+		// EBADF, and our RPC fd sits right there. Aborting kills such programs
+		// (it broke GNU hello's ./configure). macOS returns EBADF for an
+		// out-of-range target, so do the same here: the dup2 fails and the
+		// guarded fd is preserved, without taking down the process. (close.c
+		// likewise declines to abort on this guard.)
+		__simple_kprintf("dup2 to guarded FD (%d) refused with EBADF", fd_to);
+		return errno_linux_to_bsd(-LINUX_EBADF);
 	}
 
 	#if defined(__NR_dup2)
